1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:08,850
Every AI product eventually hits the same wall: API keys, quotas, and invoices.

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:16,400
Today, access to a model is gated by billing infrastructure, not by the model.

3
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:24,400
An account, an API key, a quota. An autonomous agent cannot sign up for a card.

4
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:32,400
Traffic is bursty, but the bill is a flat subscription — you pay for capacity you never use.

5
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:39,850
And every team rebuilds metering, invoicing and dunning from scratch.

6
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:47,600
The fix is already in the HTTP spec — unused since 1997.

7
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:55,000
402 Payment Required — a status code reserved for exactly this.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:04,400
x402 makes it real: the server returns a quote, the client pays, then retries with proof.

9
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:10,850
No signup. No subscription. No API key. Just a wallet and a per-request price.

10
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:18,600
Here is the whole money path. The gateway is a reverse proxy, OpenAI-compatible.

11
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:27,000
One: the caller sends a chat request. With no payment, the gateway answers 402 with a quote.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,600
Two: the client pays the quoted amount in USDC on Stellar — typically in about five seconds.

13
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:42,400
Three: the gateway verifies the transaction against Horizon and the Soroban contracts, then atomically claims the hash.

14
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:51,850
Four: the request is forwarded upstream and the caller receives the model response plus a payment receipt.

15
00:01:52,400 --> 00:02:05,400
This is not a mock-up. These are live responses from the gateway running against Stellar testnet.

16
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:13,600
An unpaid request returns a real quote: amount, asset, issuer, destination and an expiry window.

17
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:22,600
The client pays one tenth of a USDC — confirmed on-chain, and public on Stellar Expert.

18
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:31,200
Retry with that hash: HTTP 200, the model response, and a payment receipt.

19
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:40,850
Pay once, spend once — replaying the hash is refused. A forged hash is refused too: the gateway fails closed, never open.

20
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:48,000
Providers get a real operational surface, not just a proxy.

21
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Revenue, paid versus unpaid requests, and latency — with a live time-series.

22
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Routes carry their own upstream, model and pricing: flat-rate or per token.

23
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Every payment is recorded with its transaction hash, payer and ledger.

24
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Audit trail, wallet-authenticated sessions, webhooks and a persisted notification feed.

25
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,850
And escrow balances for prepaid credit.

26
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:35,000
Under the hood: a Nx monorepo with a NestJS gateway, a Next.js dashboard, and fifteen shared packages.

27
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Replay protection is layered three deep — Redis for speed, Postgres as the authority, Soroban as permanent on-chain proof.

28
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:55,000
On-chain, the verifier contract stores each used hash as its own ledger entry, so gas stays flat as history grows.

29
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:06,850
And payments are bound to a quote window, which is what stops an old transaction being replayed for free access.

30
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:15,400
Why Stellar? Because the economics only work when settlement costs almost nothing.

31
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:24,000
Fees around one hundred-thousandth of a dollar, five-second finality, native USDC, and Soroban for verification.

32
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,400
That makes a tenth-of-a-cent payment viable — which is the whole point.

33
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:36,850
API keys require trust. Subscriptions require commitment. This requires neither.

34
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:45,400
This is engineered like infrastructure: lint, unit tests, an end-to-end suite, contract tests and a size gate.

35
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:52,400
Plus gitleaks, Trivy, OSV scanning and a ZAP baseline scan on every push.

36
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:59,850
The contracts are self-tested and live on testnet. An independent audit is the gate to mainnet.

37
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:07,000
x402 LLM Gateway. Pay per request. Settle on Stellar.

38
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,400
Clone it, run the quickstart, send your first unpaid request.

39
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:16,850
MIT licensed. Built in the open.
